+
+# Critical
+
+## C-1 — `LendingMarket.upgradeTo` has no access control
+
+**Severity:** Critical · **Contract:** `LendingMarket` · **Function:** `upgradeTo` (L84–89)
+
+```solidity
+function upgradeTo(address newImplementation) external {
+    assembly { sstore(IMPLEMENTATION_SLOT, newImplementation) }
+    emit Upgraded(newImplementation);
+}
+```
+
+There is no `onlyOwner`. There is no `_authorizeUpgrade`. `MarketProxy` deliberately has no
+admin of its own — the comment on L4 of `MarketProxy.sol` says "the implementation owns the
+upgrade entry point" — so this function *is* the entire upgrade authorisation model, and it
+authorises everyone.
+
+**What the attacker gets.** Any address calls `MarketProxy.upgradeTo(evilImpl)`. The call
+delegatecalls into the market, which writes `evilImpl` into the proxy's ERC-1967 slot. The
+attacker now controls the contract at the proxy address. Because `CollateralVault.market` is
+the proxy address, `onlyMarket` is satisfied, so the attacker's implementation calls
+`vault.seize(anyUser, anyToken, attacker, fullBalance)` in a loop and takes **every deposit
+in the vault**, plus the treasury's seeded USDC sitting in the market. Total loss of protocol
+funds, one transaction, no capital required, no race to win.
+
+**Fix.** Add `onlyOwner`, and put the owner behind a multisig + timelock before launch:
+
+```solidity
+function upgradeTo(address newImplementation) external onlyOwner {
+    if (newImplementation.code.length == 0) revert NotAContract();
+    assembly { sstore(IMPLEMENTATION_SLOT, newImplementation) }
+    emit Upgraded(newImplementation);
+}
+```
+
+Better still, drop the hand-rolled proxy and inherit OpenZeppelin `UUPSUpgradeable` so
+`_authorizeUpgrade` is a compile-time obligation rather than something you have to remember.
+See also M-5 for the missing contract check.
+
