+
+## C-4 — `LendingMarket.setLiquidationThreshold` has no access control
+
+**Severity:** Critical · **Contract:** `LendingMarket` · **Function:** `setLiquidationThreshold` (L96–99)
+
+```solidity
+function setLiquidationThreshold(uint256 bps) external {
+    liquidationThreshold = bps;
+    emit LiquidationThresholdUpdated(bps);
+}
+```
+
+Third missing modifier. Unlike C-1 and C-2 this one does not hand over custody, but it does
+hand over the liquidation switch — and it is unbounded as well as unguarded (see M-6).
+
+**What the attacker gets.**
+
+- *Set it to 0.* Every position with non-zero debt now has `healthFactor == 0`. The attacker
+  immediately calls `liquidate(victim)` on each borrower, repaying their debt and collecting
+  collateral worth 105% of it. Profit is the 5% bonus on the **entire outstanding debt of the
+  market**, taken from users who were never unhealthy. On a $20M book that is $1M, and the
+  attacker can set the threshold back to 8000 in the same transaction so the book looks
+  untouched afterwards. Users additionally lose their positions and their price exposure.
+- *Set it to `type(uint256).max`.* Now nothing is ever liquidatable. Prices fall, positions go
+  underwater, and the protocol accrues bad debt it can never clear. This is a griefing action
+  with no cost to the attacker and no on-chain remedy short of an upgrade.
+
+**Fix.** `external onlyOwner`, plus bounds and an accrual, matching the shape of
+`setBorrowRate` immediately below it:
+
+```solidity
+function setLiquidationThreshold(uint256 bps) external onlyOwner {
+    if (bps == 0 || bps > BPS) revert InvalidThreshold();
+    accrueInterest();
+    liquidationThreshold = bps;
+    emit LiquidationThresholdUpdated(bps);
+}
+```
+
+Lowering the threshold makes existing positions liquidatable, so this belongs behind the
+timelock even once it is owner-gated — users need time to top up.
+
+> **Cross-cutting note on C-1/C-2/C-4.** Three of the four criticals are the same missing
+> modifier. Before Monday's fixes, grep every `external` function in `src/` and justify the
+> absence of a modifier on each one in writing. A `slither . --detect suicidal,unprotected-upgrade`
+> run and a "every state-changing external function has an access-control test" rule in CI
+> would have caught all three for free.
+
