+
+## H-2 — wstETH is priced from a stETH/USD feed
+
+**Severity:** High · **Contract:** `PriceOracle` (configuration) · **Function:** `getPrice`,
+consumed by `collateralValueUsd` and `_liquidate`
+
+The README's own deployment table says it plainly:
+
+| First listed collateral | wstETH `0x5979D7b546E38E414F7E9822514be443A4800529` |
+| Feed set for wstETH | stETH / USD `0x07C5b924399cc23c24a95c8743DE4006a32b7f2a` |
+
+wstETH is not stETH. It is the non-rebasing wrapper: one wstETH is worth
+`stEthPerToken` stETH, a value that starts at 1.0 at launch and only ever increases as
+staking rewards accrue. It is currently in the region of 1.2 and climbing. `getPrice` returns
+the stETH price and `collateralValueUsd` multiplies it straight into a wstETH balance, so
+every wstETH deposit is booked at roughly **80% of its real value**, with the gap widening
+every day the contract stays deployed.
+
+**What the unlucky user gets.** Their collateral is systematically undercounted by ~20%. They
+can borrow ~20% less than the advertised LTV, and — the part that costs them money — they are
+liquidated ~20% earlier than the parameters promise. Stacked on top of C-3, a user who
+believes they are at a safe 1.9× is booked at 1.5×, truncates to 1, and is liquidated on
+deposit. The protocol also has no mechanism to notice: the position looks legitimately
+unhealthy to every keeper and every observer.
+
+Note this is not conservative-and-therefore-safe. It is wrong in the direction that
+transfers value from depositors to liquidators, continuously.
+
+**Fix.** Two options, in order of preference:
+
+1. Use a wstETH/USD feed directly if one is live on Arbitrum for your risk parameters.
+2. Compose: read the wstETH/stETH exchange rate (either Chainlink's rate feed, or
+   `IWstETH(wstETH).stEthPerToken()` on the L1 token — on Arbitrum use the Chainlink
+   `wstETH/stETH` exchange-rate feed, not a bridged call) and multiply the stETH/USD answer
+   by it. Validate the rate the same way as any other feed: bounded, non-zero, monotonic
+   within tolerance.
+
+**This is a class, not an instance.** The README's shortlist is weETH, ezETH and USDT.
+weETH and ezETH are both exchange-rate-bearing wrappers with exactly the same trap — pricing
+either off a plain ETH/USD feed reproduces this bug at a larger scale, and ezETH additionally
+carries restaking slashing risk that a spot feed does not express. Before listing any of
+them, write down for each token: what the feed measures, what the collateral is, and the
+conversion between them. Make that a required field in `listCollateral`'s review checklist.
+
