+
+## H-3 — No Arbitrum sequencer-uptime check
+
+**Severity:** High · **Contract:** `PriceOracle` · **Function:** `getPrice`
+
+The deployment target is Arbitrum One. Chainlink's L2 integration guidance requires
+consumers to gate price reads on the L2 Sequencer Uptime Feed
+(`0xFdB631F5EE196F0ed6FAa767959853A9F217697D` on Arbitrum One), plus a grace period after
+recovery. `getPrice` does neither.
+
+**What the unlucky user gets.** The Arbitrum sequencer has gone down for hours at a time more
+than once. During an outage users cannot submit transactions through the sequencer to top up
+collateral or repay, but prices continue moving on L1 and elsewhere. The moment the sequencer
+comes back, the feed catches up in one step and a queue of liquidators — who have had the
+whole outage to prepare — executes against positions their owners had no opportunity to
+defend. Users are liquidated for a price move they were physically prevented from responding
+to.
+
+**Fix.** Read the uptime feed before every price, revert if the sequencer is down, and
+enforce a grace period (typically ~1 hour) after it returns before allowing liquidations:
+
+```solidity
+(, int256 up, uint256 startedAt, , ) = sequencerUptimeFeed.latestRoundData();
+if (up != 0) revert SequencerDown();
+if (block.timestamp - startedAt < GRACE_PERIOD) revert GracePeriod();
+```
+
+Apply the grace period to the liquidation path specifically — borrowers should still be able
+to repay and deposit during it.
+
