+
+## H-5 — `borrow(0)` grows the `borrowers` array for free, permanently DoS-ing `liquidateAll`
+
+**Severity:** High · **Contract:** `LendingMarket` · **Functions:** `_borrow` (L197–207),
+`liquidateAll` (L190–195)
+
+```solidity
+function _borrow(address borrower, uint256 amount) internal {
+    accrueInterest();
+    if (principalOf[borrower] == 0) borrowers.push(borrower);
+    principalOf[borrower] += amount * WAD / borrowIndex;
+    ...
+}
+```
+
+`borrowers` is append-only — nothing ever removes an entry, not `repay`, not `_liquidate`.
+Worse, the push is guarded on `principalOf[borrower] == 0`, which is *not* the same as "this
+address is new". Two ways it stays zero:
+
+- `borrow(0)` — `principalOf += 0`, health factor is `type(uint256).max` because debt is zero,
+  and `debtAsset.transfer(borrower, 0)` succeeds on USDC. The push executes and the guard is
+  still satisfied on the next call.
+- `borrow(1)` once `borrowIndex > 1e18` (i.e. any time after launch) — `1 * 1e18 / 1.04e18`
+  truncates to `0`. Same outcome, and it also hands over 1 wei of USDC each time (see L-2).
+
+Either way a single address can append itself to `borrowers` an unbounded number of times, at
+the cost of one array push per call, in a tight loop inside one transaction.
+
+**What the attacker gets.** `liquidateAll()` iterates `borrowers` and calls `healthFactor` on
+each — and `healthFactor` is not cheap: it loops `collateralTokens`, and makes an external
+oracle call plus a `decimals()` call per token per borrower. An attacker spends a few dollars
+of Arbitrum gas inflating `borrowers` to a length whose traversal exceeds the block gas limit,
+and `liquidateAll()` reverts with out-of-gas *forever*. There is no pagination, no removal
+function, and no way to reset the array without an upgrade.
+
+The README describes `liquidateAll` as the keeper's tool "after a large price move" — so this
+disables the protocol's primary defence at precisely the moment it matters, and the attacker
+can arm it weeks in advance for the price of lunch. Legitimate usage reaches the same wall
+more slowly: normal repay-then-reborrow cycles duplicate entries too.
+
+**Fix.** Three changes, all needed:
+
+1. Guard registration on membership, not balance: `if (!isBorrower[borrower]) { isBorrower[borrower] = true; borrowers.push(borrower); }`.
+2. Reject dust: `if (amount == 0) revert ZeroAmount();` and enforce a minimum borrow size so
+   `amount * WAD / borrowIndex` can never truncate to zero (see also L-2, L-6).
+3. **Delete `liquidateAll` entirely.** An unbounded loop over user-controlled state in a
+   permissionless function is not fixable by tuning; it is the wrong shape. Keepers should
+   compute the underwater set off-chain and call `liquidate(user)` per position, or
+   `liquidateBatch(address[] calldata users)` with a caller-supplied list. That is strictly
+   more gas-efficient and cannot be griefed.
+
