+## H-6 — `CollateralVault.withdraw` transfers before updating state
+
+**Severity:** High · **Contract:** `CollateralVault` · **Function:** `withdraw` (L44–53)
+
+```solidity
+uint256 balance = balanceOf[msg.sender][token];
+if (balance < amount) revert InsufficientBalance();
+
+IERC20(token).transfer(msg.sender, amount);          // <-- external call
+balanceOf[msg.sender][token] = balance - amount;     // <-- state written after
+ILendingMarket(market).requireHealthy(msg.sender);
+```
+
+Textbook checks-effects-interactions violation. `balance` is cached before the external call
+and written back after it, so a reentrant `withdraw` sees the original balance and passes the
+same check again. The health check at the end runs only on the outermost frame's unwind, by
+which point the tokens are gone.
+
+**What the attacker gets.** Full drain of that token from the vault — not just their own
+deposit, but every user's, since the vault pools balances in one contract. They deposit
+`X`, re-enter `n` times, and withdraw `n × X`.
+
+**Conditional on:** a collateral token that yields control during `transfer` — ERC-777, a
+token with a transfer hook, or any upgradeable token whose implementation later gains one.
+wstETH at launch has no hooks, so this is not exploitable on day one. It becomes exploitable
+the first time governance lists something exotic, and the README says the DAO has explicitly
+asked for "a path to yield-bearing and rebasing collateral". By then this code is holding real
+money and the fix requires an upgrade under pressure.
+
+Fix it now — it is two swapped lines and it removes the whole question from future listing
+reviews.
+
+**Fix.** Write state first, and add a reentrancy guard as defence in depth:
+
+```solidity
+function withdraw(address token, uint256 amount) external nonReentrant {
+    uint256 balance = balanceOf[msg.sender][token];
+    if (balance < amount) revert InsufficientBalance();
+
+    balanceOf[msg.sender][token] = balance - amount;
+    ILendingMarket(market).requireHealthy(msg.sender);
+    IERC20(token).safeTransfer(msg.sender, amount);
+
+    emit Withdrawn(msg.sender, token, amount);
+}
+```
+
+Moving `requireHealthy` above the transfer as well means the solvency check is complete before
+any value leaves, which is the property you actually want.
+
+---
+
+## H-7 — ERC20 return values are never checked
+
+**Severity:** High · **Contracts:** `LendingMarket` (L177, L204, L213), `CollateralVault` (L38, L48, L57)
+
+Every token movement in the codebase ignores the returned `bool`:
+
+```solidity
+debtAsset.transferFrom(msg.sender, address(this), amount);   // LendingMarket.repay
+debtAsset.transfer(borrower, amount);                        // LendingMarket._borrow
+debtAsset.transferFrom(msg.sender, address(this), debt);     // LendingMarket._liquidate
+IERC20(token).transferFrom(msg.sender, address(this), amount); // CollateralVault.deposit
+IERC20(token).transfer(msg.sender, amount);                  // CollateralVault.withdraw
+IERC20(token).transfer(to, amount);                          // CollateralVault.seize
+```
+
+**What the unlucky user gets.** Two distinct failure modes:
+
+- **Silent-failure tokens** (return `false` instead of reverting). `CollateralVault.deposit`
+  credits `balanceOf[msg.sender][token] += amount` after a transfer that never happened — the
+  attacker gets free collateral and borrows against it, and the loss lands on whoever
+  withdraws last. `_liquidate` marks the debt cleared and seizes collateral without receiving
+  the repayment.
+- **Non-standard tokens that return nothing.** USDT is on the README's shortlist and its
+  canonical implementation has no return value at all. The `IERC20` interface here declares
+  `returns (bool)`, so Solidity's ABI decoder expects 32 bytes of returndata and reverts when
+  it gets zero. Every USDT transfer will revert on the day it is listed — a live-DoS
+  introduced by a routine governance action.
+
+Arbitrum-native USDC (`0xaf88…5831`) reverts on failure and returns `true`, so the launch
+configuration happens to be safe. That is luck, not design, and it expires the moment the
+shortlist gets listed.
+
+**Fix.** Use OpenZeppelin `SafeERC20` (`safeTransfer` / `safeTransferFrom`) everywhere. It
+handles both the missing-return and the false-return cases. Pair it with the balance-delta
+measurement in M-2 so `deposit` credits what actually arrived.
+
+---
+
+## H-8 — Liquidation has no close factor and can strand bad debt
+
+**Severity:** High · **Contract:** `LendingMarket` · **Function:** `_liquidate` (L209–234)
+
+```solidity
+uint256 debt = debtOf(user);
+debtAsset.transferFrom(msg.sender, address(this), debt);   // liquidator pays 100% of debt
+principalOf[user] = 0;
+
+uint256 seizeUsd = debt * oracle.getPrice(address(debtAsset)) / 10 ** debtAsset.decimals();
+seizeUsd = seizeUsd * (BPS + liquidationBonus) / BPS;
+
+for (...) { ... vault.seize(user, token, msg.sender, takeUsd * unit / price); seizeUsd -= takeUsd; }
+```
+
+Two problems in the same function.
+
+**No close factor.** The liquidator must repay the borrower's *entire* debt, and the position
+is always fully closed. A borrower one basis point below the threshold — often because of C-3
+rather than any real risk — loses their whole position and pays the full 5% bonus on all of
+it. Mature markets cap a single liquidation at 50% of debt precisely so a marginal position
+gets nudged back to health rather than wiped. Beyond the user harm, the all-or-nothing design
+means positions larger than any single liquidator's USDC balance simply cannot be liquidated
+at all, so the biggest and most dangerous positions are the ones most likely to go unhandled.
+
+**The liquidator eats the shortfall.** `takeUsd = min(valueUsd, seizeUsd)`, so seizure is
+capped by what the borrower actually has. If the position is underwater — collateral worth
+less than `debt × 1.05` — the liquidator still pays 100% of `debt` and receives less than
+that in collateral. `seizeUsd` is left non-zero when the loop exits and the remainder is
+silently discarded; no event, no `badDebt` accumulator, nothing.
+
+**What this costs.** Rational liquidators simulate first and refuse any position where the
+seizure does not cover the repayment. Those positions are exactly the ones that need
+liquidating. They sit there accruing interest against collateral that keeps falling, the
+treasury absorbs the entire loss, and because there is no supply side in v1 that loss is the
+treasury's seed capital. In a fast wstETH drawdown — a stETH depeg, a slashing event — this
+turns a recoverable 10% shortfall into a permanent one.
+
+**Fix.**
+
+1. Add a close factor: cap a single liquidation at `debt * CLOSE_FACTOR / BPS` (50% is
+   standard), with a full-close carve-out below a dust threshold so tiny positions do not
+   become unliquidatable.
+2. Let the liquidator pass `repayAmount` and a `minCollateralOut`, and revert if the seizure
+   falls short — do not silently hand them a loss (see also L-7).
+3. When collateral is exhausted with `seizeUsd > 0`, recognise the shortfall explicitly:
+   accumulate it into a `badDebt` state variable and emit an event, so the treasury can see
+   insolvency accruing instead of discovering it later.
+4. Consider scaling the bonus down as health approaches `WAD`, so marginal liquidations are
+   not maximally punitive.
+
+---
+
+## H-9 — Interest is measured in blocks × 12 seconds on Arbitrum
+
+**Severity:** High · **Contract:** `LendingMarket` · **Function:** `accrueInterest` (L117–126)
+
+```solidity
+uint256 constant SECONDS_PER_BLOCK = 12;
+...
+uint256 blocksElapsed = block.number - lastAccrualBlock;
+uint256 secondsElapsed = blocksElapsed * SECONDS_PER_BLOCK;
+uint256 growth = borrowIndex * borrowRate * secondsElapsed / (BPS * 365 days);
+```
+
+On Arbitrum, `block.number` does not return the L2 block height — it returns an approximation
+of the **L1** block number, which the sequencer advances in lumps as it observes L1 and which
+can stall or jump. Hardcoding 12 seconds per block is an assumption about Ethereum mainnet
+that Arbitrum only approximately honours, and only when everything upstream is healthy.
+
+**What this costs.** Interest accrual drifts from wall-clock time in both directions. When the
+L1 view lags, borrowers get free interest-free time — a griefer can time repayments into the
+lag. When it catches up in a jump, interest lands in a discontinuous step and positions can
+cross the liquidation line without any price movement, liquidating users who were watching
+their health factor and saw no reason to act. It also silently mis-states the advertised 400
+bps to every borrower.
+
+Note `SECONDS_PER_BLOCK` is `internal constant` — correcting it later requires an upgrade, and
+because the value is embedded in the accrued index there is no clean way to retroactively fix
+a drift once it has compounded.
+
+**Fix.** Use `block.timestamp`, which on Arbitrum tracks real time closely and is not subject
+to the L1-view lag:
+
+```solidity
+uint256 public lastAccrualTime;
+
+function accrueInterest() public {
+    uint256 elapsed = block.timestamp - lastAccrualTime;
+    if (elapsed == 0) return;
+    borrowIndex += borrowIndex * borrowRate * elapsed / (BPS * 365 days);
+    lastAccrualTime = block.timestamp;
+}
+```
+
+Storage is append-only per the README, so add `lastAccrualTime` as a new slot rather than
+repurposing `lastAccrualBlock`, and initialise it in the upgrade. If you genuinely need L2
+block height for something else, `ArbSys(0x64).arbBlockNumber()` is the correct source — but
+for interest, time is what you want. See also L-4 on the accrual's dependence on call
+frequency.
+
+---
+
+# Medium
+
+## M-1 — `initialize` is front-runnable and the implementation is left uninitialised
+
+**Contract:** `LendingMarket` · **Function:** `initialize` (L56–82)
+
+`initialize` is `external` with no access control, guarded only by the `initialized` flag.
+`MarketProxy`'s constructor delegatecalls `initData`, so if deployment passes the init call
+there, the proxy is initialised atomically and is safe. If any deployment path omits
+`initData` — a scripted deploy, a redeploy, a migration — the proxy sits uninitialised and the
+first caller becomes `owner`, sets the oracle, and owns the market.
+
+Separately, the **implementation contract itself is never initialised**, so anyone can call
+`initialize` on it directly and become its owner. That is normally low-impact, but it is only
+low-impact here because `upgradeTo` is already unguarded (C-1); once C-1 is fixed this becomes
+the remaining hole in the same wall.
+
+**Fix.** Add an `initializer` modifier and disable initialisers on the implementation in its
+constructor (`_disableInitializers()` if you move to OpenZeppelin's `Initializable`).
+Make `initData` non-optional in `MarketProxy`'s constructor — revert if `initData.length == 0`.
+Add zero-address validation on all four parameters (see L-9).
+
+## M-2 — `deposit` credits the requested amount, not the amount received
+
+**Contract:** `CollateralVault` · **Function:** `deposit` (L35–42)
+
+`balanceOf[msg.sender][token] += amount` assumes the transfer delivered exactly `amount`.
+Fee-on-transfer tokens deliver less, and the vault over-credits the depositor — they borrow
+against collateral that is not there, and the shortfall is socialised onto other depositors
+because the vault pools balances.
+
+Rebasing tokens are worse and the README explicitly puts them on the roadmap. With a positive
+rebase, the vault's real balance grows but no user's `balanceOf` entry does, so the surplus is
+unattributed and claimable by whoever withdraws in the right order. With a negative rebase
+(stETH slashing), recorded balances exceed real holdings and the last withdrawers get nothing
+— and `seize` reverts on underflow, so liquidations of those positions fail entirely.
+
+**Fix.** Measure the delta:
+
+```solidity
+uint256 before = IERC20(token).balanceOf(address(this));
+IERC20(token).safeTransferFrom(msg.sender, address(this), amount);
+uint256 received = IERC20(token).balanceOf(address(this)) - before;
+balanceOf[msg.sender][token] += received;
+```
+
+That handles fee-on-transfer. Rebasing needs a different design — either a share-based
+accounting layer, or a hard rule that only non-rebasing wrappers are ever listed (wstETH, not
+stETH). Given the DAO's stated ask, write that decision down before the next listing round
+rather than discovering it in production.
+
+## M-3 — `listCollateral` performs no validation
+
+**Contract:** `LendingMarket` · **Function:** `listCollateral` (L107–114)
+
+The function pushes a token into `collateralTokens` and enables it in the vault without
+checking that a price feed exists for it, that it exposes `decimals()`, or that it is a
+contract at all. And there is no delisting path — `isCollateral` is only ever set to `true`,
+and nothing removes from `collateralTokens`.
+
+**Impact.** List a token before setting its feed and every user who deposits it has a
+`collateralValueUsd` that reverts on `FeedNotSet` — which means their `healthFactor` reverts,
+which means they cannot withdraw, cannot borrow, cannot repay, and **cannot be liquidated**.
+The same happens for any token whose `decimals()` call reverts. Because `_liquidate` and
+`collateralValueUsd` loop over all listed tokens, one bad listing can wedge every user holding
+it, with recovery only via upgrade. The lack of a delisting path means a token that later
+becomes compromised cannot be removed from the risk surface.
+
+**Fix.** Validate before pushing — require `token.code.length > 0`, require
+`oracle.getPrice(token)` to return non-zero, cache `decimals()` at listing time (see M-4), and
+cap the number of listed tokens. Add a `delistCollateral` that stops new deposits while still
+allowing withdrawal and seizure of existing balances.
+
+## M-4 — Unbounded token loop inside every health check
+
+**Contract:** `LendingMarket` · **Function:** `collateralValueUsd` (L132–139), also `_liquidate`
+
+`collateralValueUsd` iterates every listed token and, for each with a non-zero balance, makes
+two external calls: `oracle.getPrice(token)` and `IERC20(token).decimals()`. `healthFactor`
+calls it, and `healthFactor` is on the path of `borrow`, `withdraw`, `liquidate` and every
+iteration of `liquidateAll`.
+
+Cost grows linearly with the collateral list, which the README says will grow to at least four
+tokens and then further. Combined with H-5 this is the multiplier that makes `liquidateAll`
+cheap to brick. Independently, it means gas for ordinary users rises every time governance
+lists anything, and a token whose `decimals()` or feed starts reverting takes down health
+checks for every holder.
+
+**Fix.** Cache `decimals()` in a mapping at listing time — it never changes and does not
+warrant an external call per health check. Track a per-user list of *deposited* tokens so the
+loop covers only what the user actually holds, rather than the global list. Cap the total
+number of listed collaterals.
+
+## M-5 — `upgradeTo` does not check the target is a contract
+
+**Contract:** `LendingMarket` · **Function:** `upgradeTo` (L84–89)
+
+Beyond the missing access control in C-1: `sstore` accepts any address. Set the
+implementation to an EOA or an empty address and `MarketProxy`'s fallback delegatecalls into
+code-less account — which **succeeds** with empty returndata. Every call to the market then
+silently returns nothing instead of reverting. `borrow` appears to work and transfers no
+tokens; `repay` appears to work and clears no debt; the vault's `requireHealthy` call succeeds
+unconditionally, so `withdraw` lets everyone drain their collateral regardless of debt. And
+since the upgrade entry point itself now resolves to nothing, the proxy cannot be repaired.
+Permanent bricking plus an open withdrawal path.
+
+**Fix.** `if (newImplementation.code.length == 0) revert NotAContract();` before the `sstore`,
+and ideally verify the target exposes a known sentinel (`proxiableUUID()`) as UUPS does.
+
+## M-6 — No parameter bounds, no timelock, single-EOA ownership
+
+**Contract:** `LendingMarket` · **Functions:** `setBorrowRate` (L101), `setLiquidationThreshold` (L96),
+`setOracle` (L91), `upgradeTo` (L84); `PriceOracle.setFeed` (L33)
+
+Once C-1/C-2/C-4 are fixed, all of these become owner-only — which concentrates total control
+in one key. `setBorrowRate` accepts any `uint256` (a compromised owner sets 1,000,000 bps and
+liquidates the entire book on the next accrual). `setLiquidationThreshold` accepts any value
+including 0. `PriceOracle.transferOwnership` is single-step, so one typo permanently orphans
+the oracle. Nothing is timelocked, so users get no notice of a change that makes their
+position liquidatable.
+
+**Fix.** Bound every parameter at the setter (`borrowRate <= MAX_RATE`,
+`0 < liquidationThreshold <= BPS`). Put the owner behind a multisig, and route
+`setOracle` / `upgradeTo` / threshold decreases through a timelock. Use two-step ownership
+transfer (`Ownable2Step`) on both contracts. Document in the README which parameters are
+timelocked and for how long — users are taking that risk whether or not it is written down.
+
+## M-7 — `repay` transfers before updating state
+
+**Contract:** `LendingMarket` · **Function:** `repay` (L170–181)
+
+```solidity
+debtAsset.transferFrom(msg.sender, address(this), amount);
+principalOf[onBehalfOf] -= amount * WAD / borrowIndex;
+```
+
+Same CEI violation as H-6, on the debt asset rather than collateral. Not exploitable with
+Arbitrum-native USDC, which has no callbacks — but `debtAsset` is a stored variable and this
+is the pattern, not the instance. Swap the two lines. `_liquidate` (L213–214) has the
+identical ordering and the same fix.
+
+## M-8 — No pause or emergency stop
+
+**Contract:** protocol-wide
+
+There is no circuit breaker anywhere. When a feed misbehaves, a collateral token is
+compromised, or an exploit is in progress, the only lever is an upgrade — which is slow,
+requires the owner key to be reachable, and (once M-6's timelock lands) will be slower still.
+
+**Fix.** Add a `Pausable` guard on `borrow`, `borrowWithSig` and `deposit` at minimum. Do
+**not** pause `repay` or `withdraw` for healthy users — pausing exits traps users and turns an
+incident into a run. Consider a separate liquidation pause for the sequencer grace period in
+H-3.
+
+---
+
+# Low / Informational
+
+**L-1 — Debt can never be fully repaid (`repay`, L178).** `debt = floor(P × I / W)` then
+`P -= floor(debt × W / I)` leaves up to 1 unit of scaled principal behind. The borrower always
+carries dust debt and can never see a zero balance. Round the repayment amount up when the
+caller is clearing the full position, or zero `principalOf` outright when `amount == debt`.
+
+**L-2 — Sub-index borrows record zero principal (`_borrow`, L201).** Once `borrowIndex > 1e18`,
+`amount * WAD / borrowIndex` truncates to zero for `amount == 1`, so 1 wei of USDC is borrowed
+with no debt recorded. The direct value leak is negligible (gas costs far exceed 1e-6 USDC),
+but it is the mechanism behind H-5's free array growth. Enforce a minimum borrow amount and
+revert if the computed principal delta is zero.
+
+**L-3 — `10 ** (18 - feed.decimals())` underflows for feeds with more than 18 decimals
+(`PriceOracle.getPrice`, L44).** Reverts rather than misprices, so the impact is a DoS on that
+token only. Chainlink USD feeds are 8 decimals so the launch config is fine. Add the explicit
+`if (d > 18) revert` from H-1's fix.
+
+**L-4 — Accrual is permissionless and frequency-dependent (`accrueInterest`, L117).** `growth`
+is proportional to the current `borrowIndex`, so the index compounds once per call. Anyone can
+call `accrueInterest()` in a loop to compound more often and push borrowers toward
+liquidation. At 400 bps the maximum achievable difference between discrete and continuous
+compounding is under 0.1%/year, so this is not economically meaningful today — but it scales
+with `borrowRate`, so revisit if rates ever go materially higher.
+
+**L-5 — ETH sent to the proxy is unrecoverable (`MarketProxy.receive`, L38).** The proxy
+accepts ETH and `LendingMarket` has no sweep function. Remove `receive()` and make `fallback()`
+non-payable — the market has no use for ETH.
+
+**L-6 — No minimum position size.** Dust positions cost more in gas to liquidate than the
+bonus is worth, so they are never liquidated and accumulate as bad debt. Enforce a minimum
+borrow (a few hundred USDC) and require positions to be fully closed rather than left below it.
+
+**L-7 — Liquidators have no slippage protection (`_liquidate`).** The liquidator commits to
+repaying `debtOf(user)` as computed at execution time and receives collateral at whatever price
+the oracle reports in the same transaction, with no minimum-out parameter. A price move between
+submission and inclusion can turn a profitable liquidation into a loss. Add `maxRepay` and
+`minCollateralOut` arguments — this is folded into H-8's fix.
+
+**L-8 — No test suite.** The README says the suite "lands with the audit fixes". Nearly every
+finding above is a single unit test: an access-control test on each external function would
+have caught C-1, C-2 and C-4; one boundary assertion on `healthFactor` would have caught C-3.
+Land the tests **with** the fixes, not after, and add invariant tests for
+"sum of user balances == vault token balance", "no healthy position is liquidatable" and
+"total debt never decreases without a repayment".
+
+**L-9 — No zero-address validation in `initialize` (L56).** `owner_`, `oracle_`, `vault_` and
+`debtAsset_` are all accepted unchecked. A zero `owner_` permanently locks every owner-gated
+function; a zero `oracle_` bricks all health checks. Cheap to add, unrecoverable if missed.
+
+**L-10 — Liquidation seizes in listing order (`_liquidate`, L219).** The liquidator always
+receives whatever is first in `collateralTokens`, with no ability to choose. Predictable and
+unfair across collateral types, and it drains the earliest-listed collateral
+disproportionately. Let the liquidator specify which collateral to seize.
+
+---
+
+## Notes and limits
+
+- **Not verified:** deployment ordering. `CollateralVault.market` is `immutable`, so the vault
+  must be constructed with the **proxy** address, not the implementation's — but the proxy's
+  constructor needs the vault address in `initData`. That is circular unless you
+  counterfactually compute one address (CREATE2) or deploy the vault second and pass it in via
+  `initialize`. There are no deployment scripts in the repo, so I could not check which path
+  you intend. Confirm before launch: getting this wrong makes `seize` permanently revert, which
+  means no liquidations at all.
+- **Not verified:** the on-chain identity of the feed addresses in the README. I took the
+  README's own labels at face value — H-2 rests on the table saying "Feed set for wstETH:
+  stETH / USD", which is a configuration error regardless of which contract sits at that
+  address. Verify each feed's `description()` on Arbitrum before deployment and record the
+  expected string in a deployment test.
+- **Out of scope:** the treasury's off-chain seeding process, keeper infrastructure, and the
+  economic soundness of the 8000/500/400 bps parameter set against wstETH volatility. The
+  parameters are plausible but a risk analysis is a separate exercise from this code review.
+- **`forge build` was not run** — the repo has no `lib/` and no dependencies vendored, so the
+  audit is a source-level review only.

